WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.649 --> 00:00:11.710
Ever since man landed on the moon, we have
been dreaming of colonising it.

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:15.840
We have the technology to get there, the only
thing that is standing in our way is the cost

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:17.990
of sending materials to the moon.

00:00:17.990 --> 00:00:22.840
While companies like Space X and Blue Origin
are aiming to reduce the cost of rocket launches.

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:27.160
The real challenge will be using the materials
available to us on the moon.

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:32.940
Luckily for us the moon has no shortage of
raw materials for us to exploit, but new technologies

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:36.440
need to be developed in order to extract them
efficiently.

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:41.260
This would give the lunar colony the materials
necessary to build and expand, but more importantly

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:46.010
stimulate a lunar economy where it can sell
the materials it gathers, like rocket fuel

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:47.379
and rare metals.

00:00:47.379 --> 00:00:50.290
So what materials are available in the lunar
soil?

00:00:50.290 --> 00:00:53.360
The elements available differ with regions
on the moon.

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:58.900
Water will be a high priority resource, and
in 2009, India’s Chandrayaan-1 lunar satellite

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:01.230
detected ice at the moon's poles.

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:05.530
The poles are likely the best candidate for
our first moon colonists, but not just for

00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:06.540
the water.

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:09.890
There are regions on the poles that receive
almost continuous sunlight like the ridges

00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:11.580
of the Shackleton crater.

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:15.620
Allowing these early colonists to bring solar
panels with them and have a continuous supply

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:16.620
of energy.

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:21.240
And energy will be in high demand, as all
extraction techniques will need to be electrified.

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:25.390
The most efficient method of extraction of
the lunar ice, will be to simply collect the

00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:29.950
lunar soil containing the ice and place it
into a microwave oven, where it will evaporate

00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:33.190
at very low temperatures thanks to the vacuum
on the moon.

00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:35.950
The vapour will then be condensed and collected.[1]

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:40.250
Our next commodity that will be high in demand
is oxygen, and surprisingly oxygen is one

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:44.200
of the most abundant elements on the moon
[2], but it is trapped in the form of oxides.

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:49.270
Like Iron Oxide, aluminium oxide, titanium
oxide, and silicon oxide.

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:52.909
These are all valuable materials, and if we
can create oxygen as a by product of their

00:01:52.909 --> 00:01:56.320
extraction that just makes it all the more
worthwhile.

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:59.340
Iron will be a vital material for any lunar
colony.

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:04.229
Here on earth it so vital for modern society
that its production and utilisation is a clear

00:02:04.229 --> 00:02:06.280
indicator of economic growth.

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:08.750
It reinforces our buildings through rebar.

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:12.560
it holds the weight of our electrical grid
on its shoulders and it bridges our oceans

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:17.840
and rivers, AND it is heavy making it far
too expensive to transport to the moon

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:22.049
To produce Iron with current methods we first
need to mine limestone, coal and the iron

00:02:22.049 --> 00:02:23.450
ore itself.

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:28.209
The coal is refined into coke, by baking off
impurities like tar and water in a coke oven,

00:02:28.209 --> 00:02:30.930
which itself is a energy intensive process.

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:34.840
This coke is then use as a fuel source in
a blast furnace to reduce our iron ore by

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:39.810
producing carbon monoxide, which reacts with
the iron oxide ore to form iron and carbon

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:45.090
dioxide, while the limestone breaks down to
carbon dioxide and calcium oxide, which reacts

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:46.840
with impurities in the ore.

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:52.099
The pig iron produced then needs further refinement
in an oxygen furnace to produce steel.

00:02:52.099 --> 00:02:56.109
This process will need to be streamlined for
any lunar colony, as shipping heavy fossil

00:02:56.109 --> 00:03:01.250
fuels to the moon, purely to extract iron
from its ore is simply not an option.

00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:07.010
To add some incentive to develop this technology,
this complete process contributes 1.7 gigatonnes

00:03:07.010 --> 00:03:09.969
of carbon dioxide to the atmosphere annually.

00:03:09.969 --> 00:03:12.220
Five percent of total global emissions.

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:13.220
[3]

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:17.750
Developing a fully electrified version will
allow us to manufacture emissions free steel.

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:21.530
NASA offered a quarter-million dollar prize
to the first research team that could develop

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:27.790
a reasonable method to process these resources
on the moon while producing oxygen as a bi-product.

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:32.700
Donald Sadoway [4], a Professor of Materials
Chemistry at MIT, proposed using an electrolytic

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:33.700
cell.

00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:37.329
This cell would use an electric current to
separate the metals and release the oxygen

00:03:37.329 --> 00:03:38.769
from its bonds.

00:03:38.769 --> 00:03:39.769
[5]

00:03:39.769 --> 00:03:43.540
Aluminium production already uses electrolysis
to refine aluminium oxide.

00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:48.909
Aluminium oxide has a very high melting point
of 2072 degrees celsius, and so to reduce

00:03:48.909 --> 00:03:54.159
the energy required the aluminium oxide is
instead dissolved in molten cryolite, which

00:03:54.159 --> 00:03:59.609
has a lower melting point of 1012 degrees
celcius and thus this method reduces energy

00:03:59.609 --> 00:04:01.659
requirements substantially.

00:04:01.659 --> 00:04:06.139
This molten mixture is then placed in a steel
case, which is lined with a graphite cathode,

00:04:06.139 --> 00:04:08.269
the anodes are also made out of graphite.

00:04:08.269 --> 00:04:13.499
When an electric current flows aluminium ions
form at the cathode where they gain electrons

00:04:13.499 --> 00:04:17.290
and sink to the bottom to form molten aluminium
that can be tapped off.

00:04:17.290 --> 00:04:21.459
Meanwhile the oxygen ions accumulate at the
graphite anodes where they react with the

00:04:21.459 --> 00:04:24.780
carbon in the graphite to form carbon dioxide.

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:28.120
This will be the general process for metal
extraction on the moon, but

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:32.870
graphite is obviously off the table as a material
for our anode and cathode, if we want to produce

00:04:32.870 --> 00:04:37.440
oxygen as it produces carbon dioxide and is
consumed in the process.

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:41.290
Carbon is in short supply on the moon though,
so this may be a clever way of producing carbon

00:04:41.290 --> 00:04:43.259
dioxide on the moon for plant growth.

00:04:43.259 --> 00:04:49.120
In a paper published in 2012, Sadoway and
his research partner Antoine Allanore conclude

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:54.349
that a chromium alloy anode is the best choice,
as a protective and conductive layer of chromium

00:04:54.349 --> 00:04:57.449
and aluminium oxides form on its surface.

00:04:57.449 --> 00:05:01.580
This allows it to trade electrons with the
negatively charged oxygen ions without being

00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:06.530
heavily corroded in the process and oxygen
is formed instead of carbon dioxide [6].

00:05:06.530 --> 00:05:10.180
This will be an incredibly energy intensive
process, and it’s one of the main stumbling

00:05:10.180 --> 00:05:14.330
blocks currently stopping it from being used
here on earth for iron refinement.

00:05:14.330 --> 00:05:18.539
It’s also one of the main reasons aluminium
is more expensive than iron.

00:05:18.539 --> 00:05:23.039
Generating electricity on the moon will initially
rely on lightweight solar panels shipped to

00:05:23.039 --> 00:05:27.550
the moon, but for a moon economy to grow it
will need to be capable of expanding its own

00:05:27.550 --> 00:05:29.300
electrical grid.

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:32.889
Aluminium is readily available on the moon,
and it can be used for wiring like it is here

00:05:32.889 --> 00:05:35.440
on earth for high voltage power lines.

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:39.889
The primary material used in solar panels
is silicon, which abundant on the moon, and

00:05:39.889 --> 00:05:42.250
applications go far beyond just solar panels.

00:05:42.250 --> 00:05:46.330
It can be used to make glass and it’s used
to alloy with aluminium and iron.

00:05:46.330 --> 00:05:50.479
While solar panels primary material is silicon,
the silicon we find in solar powers needs

00:05:50.479 --> 00:05:54.970
to be incredibly pure, and it’s production
also requires a carbon supply in the form

00:05:54.970 --> 00:05:56.099
of coke or charcoal.

00:05:56.099 --> 00:06:00.860
I couldn’t find any technologies being developed
to free its production from these feedstocks,

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:05.129
which are not available on the moon, and ultimately
I think sending lightweight solar panels from

00:06:05.129 --> 00:06:10.310
Earth to the Moon will probably be more economical
until new technologies are developed.

00:06:10.310 --> 00:06:15.129
The moon has a rich supply of helium 3 however,
thanks solar winds, and if we manage to develop

00:06:15.129 --> 00:06:16.970
nuclear fusion technologies.

00:06:16.970 --> 00:06:21.009
Generating power on the moon will become vastly
easier, and allow the moon to develop a healthy

00:06:21.009 --> 00:06:24.550
economy harvesting this gas and sending it
back to earth.

00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:29.289
Ultimately a self sustaining moon colony will
depend completely on its ability to harvest

00:06:29.289 --> 00:06:33.560
and make use of the materials available to
it, and thankfully the technologies being

00:06:33.560 --> 00:06:38.419
developed for that purpose have uses here
right on earth for freeing us from dependency

00:06:38.419 --> 00:06:39.879
on fossil fuels.

00:06:39.879 --> 00:06:44.800
As always striving to do what is difficult,
challenges humans to develop and grow as a

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:45.800
species.

00:06:45.800 --> 00:06:47.569
This is why the space program is important.

00:06:47.569 --> 00:06:51.169
Challenging ourselves is a core part of being
human, it allows us to discover things about

00:06:51.169 --> 00:06:56.169
ourselves and learn what we are really capable
of, and so it’s important for you to continually

00:06:56.169 --> 00:06:58.939
